{
  "teamId": 119,
  "year": 2023,
  "narrative": "The 2023 Los Angeles Dodgers opened the year with a stumble — Kyle Lewis launched an Opening Day crusher off Alex Vesia to set an ominous tone — but the roster proved too deep and too talented to stay down for long. By mid-April, walk-off heroes like David Peralta were rescuing games in dramatic fashion, and Chris Taylor emerged as an unlikely catalyst, delivering big home runs and even two grand slams to keep the offense humming. The rotation and bullpen had their rough nights, and June was a .500 grind, but the Dodgers were accumulating wins through sheer organizational depth.\n\nAugust was when the season truly announced itself. The Dodgers went a stunning 25-5, one of the best months in modern franchise history, turning a competitive but imperfect summer into a dominant wire-to-wire statement. J.D. Martinez, Will Smith, and Max Muncy provided the big bats while the bullpen — despite occasional hiccups from Evan Phillips and Alex Vesia — generally locked games down. The team finished 100-62, scoring 906 runs and slugging 249 home runs, a legitimate juggernaut on paper.\n\nSeptember brought some turbulence — Alex Vesia surrendered a three-run blast to Orlando Arcia in a costly loss, Evan Phillips was touched up in key spots, and the offense sputtered in a few series — but the Dodgers had already built enough of a cushion to cruise into October. The postseason dream, however, dissolved quickly and painfully against the Arizona Diamondbacks in the NLDS. Clayton Kershaw was roughed up in Game 1, Freddie Freeman grounded into double plays at the worst moments, and Arizona's surprising lineup — led by Ketel Marte and an unlikely Geraldo Perdomo home run — kept the Dodgers off balance throughout. A 100-win season, one of the finest regular campaigns in baseball, ended in a stunning first-round exit, leaving Los Angeles to wonder what might have been.",
